summary:
Asthma is a chronic condition in which the airways become inflamed and narrowed. This makes breathing difficult. When asthma suddenly worsens (known as an asthma attack or exacerbation), it can severely impair breathing and often requires immediate medical attention.
Such exacerbations can be triggered by things such as allergies, air pollution or infections.
Doctors usually check for asthma using breathing tests and questions about symptoms.
However, researchers are also investigating specific substances in the body, known as biomarkers. These can help predict when your asthma might worsen. An important biomarker is the FeNO  measurement (a gas measured when you breathe out).
Biomarkers such as interleukin 33 (IL-33) are substances produced by cells in the airways when they are irritated or infected, which can increase inflammation and trigger asthma attacks. Doctors therefore hope that measuring these substances will help to detect asthma attacks at an early stage – and perhaps offer new treatment options in the future.
Most people with asthma respond well to inhaled medications that reduce inflammation. In some cases, there are also new biological therapies that specifically block certain substances such as IL-5, IL-13 or IL-33.
The most appropriate treatment depends on the type of inflammation present in your body. This can be determined by testing for biomarkers.
Biomarkers can be measured in saliva. Saliva tests are easier and painless – you can even submit the sample at home.
Researchers are currently investigating whether some blood tests can be replaced by saliva tests in the future. This could make it much easier to control asthma.
These new findings can help to better control asthma, prevent attacks and improve quality of life. 
The aim is to see whether certain substances (biomarkers, e.g. IL-33) in saliva change in asthma and can help to predict asthma attacks or guide treatment.
An additional goal is to test whether saliva can be easily tested at home to detect inflammation.
First visit:
We collect information about you (e.g. age, weight, medications);
We measure your vital signs (blood pressure, pulse, etc.);
There is a physical examination and lung function tests;
Saliva samples are taken;
Blood samples are taken in the amount required for routine laboratory testing.
Saliva samples at home:
In the event of acute asthma attacks: daily for the first 7 days;
After that: weekly for 5 weeks for all participants;
You will also complete short questionnaires.
Interim check-up (day 42):
We repeat the examinations and saliva samples;
We check whether you have had any side effects or new asthma attacks;
Treatment can be adjusted if necessary.
Final visit (day 84):
Final examinations, questionnaires and samples;
Check for side effects and asthma attacks;
After that, your doctor will take over your further care.

